          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: Entrepreneurial Ecosystem + Matchmaking</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students understand that entrepreneurship is not a solo sport. They can map their own ecosystem and identify specific people and resources they should be connecting with.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Think about someone outside this classroom — a person in your community, your family, your neighborhood — who is doing something entrepreneurial. What are they doing?&quot; 3-4 students share.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Ecosystem + Matchmaking</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;In Unit 1 you found a problem, built a BMC, drafted a May-B-P, and pitched. Now we go deeper. The next phase is about building the relationships and resources that make your idea real.&quot;</p><p>&quot;You do not build a business alone. You build it with an ecosystem.&quot;</p><p>Talking Point 1 — What Is an Entrepreneurial Ecosystem? (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;An entrepreneurial ecosystem is the network of people, organizations, and resources that support entrepreneurs in a given community.&quot;</p><p>Draw on the board — 5 rings around the student:</p><p>Mentors — people who have done what you want to do</p><p>Peers — other entrepreneurs at your stage</p><p>Customers — the people whose problem you are solving</p><p>Resources — money, tools, spaces, programs</p><p>Champions — people who will open doors for you</p><p>Say: &quot;Most people only think about customers. But the strongest entrepreneurs invest in all five rings. Each one accelerates your growth.&quot;</p><p>Talking Point 2 — Matchmaking (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Matchmaking is the process of intentionally connecting yourself with the right people at the right time — not waiting for it to happen.&quot;</p><p>&quot;There are three questions that drive matchmaking:&quot;</p><p>Who has solved a version of my problem before?</p><p>Who knows my potential customer better than I do?</p><p>Who has resources or access that I do not have yet?</p><p>&quot;Once you know the answers, your job is to find those people and show up with something to offer — not just something to ask for.&quot;</p><p>Talking Point 3 — Walk, Talk, Show Up Revisited (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;You used Walk, Talk, Show Up in Unit 1 to find problems. Now we use it again — this time to find people and resources.&quot;</p><p>&quot;Same three moves, different lens:&quot;</p><p>Walk — move through spaces where your ecosystem exists. Farmers markets, business districts, community events, makerspaces.</p><p>Talk — have real conversations. Not pitching. Asking. &quot;How did you start? What do you wish you had known?&quot;</p><p>Show Up — be present in spaces where your ecosystem gathers. Volunteer. Contribute. Be seen.</p><p>Close: &quot;This week we build your ecosystem map and identify your top 3 matchmaking targets — the three people you most need to connect with right now.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Ecosystem Mapping (First Draft)</h4><p>Students draw their ecosystem on paper — themselves in the center, five rings around them. Fill in what they know already.</p><p>Walk the room. Push: &quot;Who in your life fits in the Mentors ring? Even if you are not connected yet — who should be there?&quot;</p><p>After 15 minutes: &quot;Circle the three biggest gaps in your ecosystem. Those are your matchmaking targets.&quot;</p><p>Pairs share. Ask: &quot;What does your partner&#x27;s ecosystem have that yours does not? Could you connect them to someone in your network?&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview Tuesday: &quot;Tomorrow we go deeper on the Discovery Tracker and finalize your ecosystem map with your matchmaking targets named.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Discovery Walk Debrief + Ecosystem Mapping</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students finalize ecosystem maps, identify 3 matchmaking targets by name (or description), and reconnect their Discovery Tracker observations to real people they could talk to.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Who is one person outside this room who would be a valuable addition to your ecosystem — and why?&quot; Around the room fast.</p><h4>7:35–8:00 AM — Ecosystem Map: Final Version</h4><p>Students complete their ecosystem map. This time: every person or organization they identify must have a name or a specific description — not &quot;a mentor&quot; but &quot;someone who runs a food business in South Bend.&quot;</p><p>Top 3 matchmaking targets: Name them. Write one sentence on why each one and how you would Show Up for them first.</p><h4>8:00–8:30 AM — Discovery Tracker + Ecosystem Connection</h4><p>Students review their Discovery Tracker from Unit 1. For each strong observation: &quot;Is there someone in my ecosystem who is already trying to solve this? Who could I Talk to about it?&quot;</p><p>Add a new column to the tracker: &quot;Who should I talk to about this?&quot;</p><h4>8:30–8:50 AM — Outreach Planning</h4><p>Using the outreach skills from Week 5: students draft one outreach message to one of their three matchmaking targets.</p><p>Use AI to help draft. Then edit until it sounds like them.</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets. Prepare for Wednesday.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Discovery Deep Dive</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What we heard&quot; | &quot;Does this person belong in someone&#x27;s ecosystem map?&quot;</p><p>Ask: &quot;Based on what you heard — which ring of the ecosystem did this speaker represent for you?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements:</p><p>One specific thing you heard or observed</p><p>One question it sparked</p><p>A genuine, specific thank you</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Discovery Tracker Work + 1-on-1s</h4><p>Students strengthen Discovery Tracker entries and ecosystem maps. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;Who are your three matchmaking targets? Have you reached out yet?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Founder Fight + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Tell your partner: who is one person you are going to reach out to this week from your ecosystem map and what will you say?&quot; 90 seconds.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Founder Fight (Week 7 — Rotation: Founder Fight)</h4><p>Same format as before. Two students, assigned businesses, 60 seconds each, class votes. This week: tie businesses to ecosystem themes.</p><p>Round 1: A solo freelance graphic designer vs. a small design agency with 3 employees</p><p>Round 2: An entrepreneur who bootstraps with no outside help vs. one who actively builds a mentor network</p><p>Round 3: A business that markets only online vs. one that relies entirely on community relationships</p><p>Debrief: &quot;Which of these businesses has the stronger ecosystem? Why does that matter?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Show me your ecosystem map. Who is missing? Who have you already reached out to?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What is the biggest gap in your entrepreneurial ecosystem right now?</p><p>Who is one person you plan to connect with before next Friday?</p><p>How does your ecosystem connect back to your May-B-P?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 8</h4><p>Week 8: Creativity + Design Thinking in Action. &quot;Next week we learn how the best entrepreneurs use creative problem-solving — not as a personality trait, but as a repeatable method.&quot;</p>"
