          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: Customer Personas + TAM/SAM/SOM</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students can build a specific customer persona and explain the three rings of market sizing — TAM, SAM, SOM.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Describe your ideal customer in one sentence — be specific. Not &#x27;teenagers.&#x27; Age range, location, situation, what they are dealing with right now.&quot; Around the room.</p><p>Write 4-5 on the board. Note which are specific vs. generic.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Personas + TAM</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;There is a big difference between knowing who your customer is and understanding your customer. A persona forces you to get specific — to give your customer a face, a name, a daily life.&quot;</p><p>Talking Point 1 — Customer Personas (10 min)</p><p>A persona has several layers:</p><p>Demographics — name, age, location, job, income, family situation</p><p>Goals — what they are trying to achieve in the area your business addresses</p><p>Frustrations — what is getting in their way right now</p><p>Behaviors — how they currently solve the problem (badly)</p><p>Watering holes — where they spend time online and offline</p><p>Quote — one sentence they might actually say about their problem</p><p>Walk through a full example: Name: Maria. Age: 34. Location: South Bend. Job: Preschool teacher. Income: $38,000/year. Goal: Feed her family healthy meals on a tight budget without spending her entire Sunday cooking. Frustration: Meal prep services are too expensive, grocery store is 20 minutes away, no time to plan. Behavior: Buys frozen meals even though she hates them. Quote: &quot;I just want food that is real and doesn&#x27;t cost me an hour every night.&quot;</p><p>Say: &quot;Notice how specific that is. Now I know exactly what to build, where to find her, and what to say when I do.&quot;</p><p>Talking Point 2 — TAM, SAM, SOM (12 min)</p><p>TAM — Total Addressable Market: everyone who could theoretically buy your product. &quot;The full pool.&quot;</p><p>SAM — Serviceable Addressable Market: the portion you can actually reach given your current geography, channels, and capabilities. &quot;The pool you can swim in.&quot;</p><p>SOM — Serviceable Obtainable Market: the realistic slice you can capture in year 1-3. &quot;The pool you will actually swim in.&quot;</p><p>Example: TAM = all budget-conscious families in the US who want meal prep help (~$50B market). SAM = families in South Bend reachable through local channels (~15,000 households). SOM = families I can realistically serve in year 1 (starting with 20, growing to 100).</p><p>Say: &quot;Investors want to see a big TAM — it shows the opportunity is real. But they believe your SOM — that is where your credibility lives. A realistic SOM you can defend is worth more than an inflated TAM you cannot explain.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Persona First Draft</h4><p>Students fill out a full persona for their primary customer. All sections. Give them a name.</p><p>Walk the room. Find the most specific thing on each persona — reinforce it. Find the vaguest — push: &quot;What does this person actually do when this problem happens?&quot;</p><p>After 25 minutes: Pairs read each other&#x27;s. &quot;Does this feel like a real person?&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview Tuesday: &quot;Tomorrow you calculate TAM, SAM, and SOM using real data.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Persona Workshop + Market Size Calculation</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Read your persona&#x27;s quote to the room.&quot; Every student, 10 seconds each.</p><h4>7:35–8:00 AM — Persona Refinement</h4><p>AI prompt: &quot;Here is my customer persona [paste it]. What questions is this persona NOT answering that would be important before building a product for them?&quot;</p><p>Add what is missing. Make it more specific where AI identifies gaps.</p><h4>8:00–8:45 AM — TAM/SAM/SOM Calculation</h4><p>AI research prompt: &quot;Help me estimate the TAM for a business that [describe]. What data sources should I use to find real market size numbers?&quot;</p><p>Walk the room. Push for real data — census data, industry reports, local statistics. &quot;What is your SOM in year 1? Does it match your May-B-P capacity?&quot;</p><p>Students document: TAM (with source), SAM (with reasoning), SOM (with rationale). These go in the business portfolio.</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Persona + Market Refinement</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What we heard&quot; | &quot;Does this change my persona or market size thinking?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific observation, one question it sparked, a genuine thank you.</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Persona + Market Size Work Day</h4><p>Students finalize persona and TAM/SAM/SOM. These feed into the pitch deck. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;Walk me through your numbers. Where did you get them? Why is your SOM realistic?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Founder Fight + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;In one sentence — who is your persona and what is the one thing they most need?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Founder Fight (Week 10 — Rotation: Founder Fight)</h4><p>Market-sizing edition:</p><p>Round 1: A business that serves high school students only vs. one that serves all people under 25</p><p>Round 2: A local South Bend business vs. the same business scaled nationally from day 1</p><p>Round 3: A niche product for competitive athletes vs. a general fitness product for everyone</p><p>Debrief: &quot;Which has the bigger TAM? Which has the more realistic SOM? Are those the same business?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Does your persona match your May-B-P target? If your May-B-P reaches 10 people, are they the right 10?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What is the most specific thing about your persona that changed how you think about your product?</p><p>What surprised you about your market size numbers?</p><p>How does knowing your SOM change what you do first?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 11</h4><p>Week 11: Customer Discovery Interviews. &quot;Next week you go talk to real customers — face to face or voice to voice. You will learn more in one real conversation than in a week of research.&quot;</p>"
