          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: Distribution Channels + Competitive Landscape</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students can name and explain their top 3 distribution channels and explain what makes a channel right for their specific customer.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;How did you find out about the last thing you bought? Walk me through it.&quot; 4-5 students share. Write the channels on the board.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Channels + Competition</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;You can build a great product, price it perfectly, and target the right customer — and still fail if you are trying to reach them in the wrong place. The channel is how you reach your customer. The right channel is where they already are, already trust information, and are already making decisions.&quot;</p><p>Types of Channels (8 min)</p><p>Direct channels:</p><p>In person: events, markets, door-to-door, community spaces</p><p>Direct outreach: DMs, texts, calls, emails</p><p>Your own social media: your audience</p><p>Indirect channels:</p><p>Referrals: existing customers tell others</p><p>Partnerships: another business sends you customers</p><p>Marketplaces: Etsy, eBay, Facebook Marketplace</p><p>Press and content: stories, podcasts, blogs</p><p>Say: &quot;The best channel is the one your specific customer already uses and trusts. Go back to your persona — where does your customer spend time?&quot;</p><p>&quot;The worst approach: try every channel and be mediocre everywhere. Pick one or two and be excellent there first.&quot;</p><p>Competitive Research (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;You are never the only solution. Even if there is no direct competitor, your customer has a workaround — and that workaround is your real competition.&quot;</p><p>How to research:</p><p>Google the problem from your customer&#x27;s perspective — what comes up?</p><p>Search your customer&#x27;s watering holes — what do people recommend?</p><p>Look at review sites, Reddit, local Facebook groups — who are people already paying?</p><p>AI prompt: &quot;What are the existing solutions for [your problem]? What do customers complain about most?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Channel Mapping</h4><p>Students list all possible channels, score each on (1) how well it reaches their persona and (2) feasibility right now. Pick top 3.</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;Your persona spends time at [place from their persona]. Is that channel on your list?&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview Tuesday: &quot;Tomorrow you research your competition and build your SWOT.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Competitive Research + SWOT Analysis</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Name one competitor — direct or indirect — and what they do better than you right now.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:10 AM — Competitive Research</h4><p>Research 2-3 competitors. For each: name, offer and price, target customer, what they do well (positive reviews), what they do badly (negative reviews), how your business compares.</p><h4>8:10–8:50 AM — SWOT Analysis</h4><p>Strengths and Weaknesses = internal (your business now). Opportunities and Threats = external (market, competition, environment).</p><p>Strengths: what do you have that competitors don&#x27;t? Your Bird-in-Hand is a clue.</p><p>Weaknesses: what do your discovery interviews say you still don&#x27;t have?</p><p>Opportunities: what gap in the competitive landscape did you find?</p><p>Threats: what could kill this — market shift, a competitor copying you, cost changes?</p><p>After: &quot;Circle your biggest Strength and biggest Opportunity. That intersection is your competitive advantage.&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + SWOT + Channel Planning</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What channels did this person use?&quot; | &quot;What was their competitive advantage?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific observation, one question it sparked, a genuine thank you.</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Channel Plan + SWOT Refinement</h4><p>Students finalize channel plan (top 3 with one action each) and refine SWOT. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;What is your competitive advantage — the specific thing that makes a customer choose you over their current workaround?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Founder Fight + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence: your competitive advantage.&quot; Around the room. Note which are specific vs. vague.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Founder Fight (Week 13 — Rotation: Founder Fight)</h4><p>Round 1: A business that sells only through social media vs. one that sells only in person</p><p>Round 2: A business with no competitors vs. one with 10 established competitors</p><p>Round 3: A business with one strong channel vs. one that tries to be everywhere</p><p>Debrief: &quot;What makes a channel strategy strong vs. scattered? What does having competition actually signal about a market?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Walk me through your SWOT. What opportunity excites you most? What threat worries you most?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What did you learn about your competition that changed how you see your business?</p><p>What is your most important channel and one action you will take in it this week?</p><p>What is your one clearest competitive advantage right now?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 14</h4><p>Week 14: Sales Strategy — Processes, People, and Funnels. &quot;Next week we build an actual sales process.&quot;</p>"
