          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: Value Proposition + Pricing Models</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students can write a clear value proposition and explain why their pricing is what it is.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;What do you currently charge — or plan to charge — and why that number?&quot; Around the room. Listen for: &quot;I guessed,&quot; &quot;I looked at competitors,&quot; &quot;I don&#x27;t know.&quot; Most are guessing.</p><p>Say: &quot;By Thursday you will have a defensible price — not a guess.&quot;</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Value Proposition + Pricing</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Your value proposition answers: why should someone choose you? Not your category — specifically you, this version, at this price. And your price is not just a number. It is a signal — it tells the customer who you are, who you are for, and what the experience will be.&quot;</p><p>Value Proposition Framework (10 min)</p><p>Format: &quot;We help [specific customer] [do/achieve/avoid something] by [your unique approach] — unlike [alternative], which [limitation].&quot;</p><p>Example: &quot;We help busy working parents in South Bend get fresh, affordable meal kits delivered to their door by partnering with local farms — unlike national meal kit services, which are too expensive and use produce shipped from across the country.&quot;</p><p>The &quot;so what?&quot; test: after your value proposition, someone asks &quot;so what?&quot; If you can&#x27;t answer in one sentence, it is not specific enough.</p><p>Bad: &quot;We help people eat better.&quot; — So what?</p><p>Good: &quot;We help single parents spend less than $50 a week on healthy dinners without spending Sunday cooking.&quot; — So what? &quot;More time with their kids and more money in their pocket.&quot;</p><p>Pricing Models (12 min)</p><p>Cost-Plus: Cost per unit × (1 + margin %). Example: costs $12, want 50% margin → price $18. Use for: physical products with clear, consistent costs.</p><p>Value-Based: Price based on what it is worth to the customer. Example: A tax preparer charges $300, saves customer $200 vs. H&amp;R Block. Use for: services, expertise, time-saving solutions.</p><p>Competitive: Price relative to market — above (premium), at (competing on quality), or below (volume/penetration). Warning on below: hard to raise prices later.</p><p>Subscription/Recurring: Customer pays a regular fee for ongoing access. Use for: ongoing services, communities, content. Advantage: predictable revenue.</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Value Prop Draft + Pricing Analysis</h4><p>Students write 3 versions of their value proposition. Apply the &quot;so what?&quot; test. Pick the strongest. Then: which pricing model fits your business? Calculate your price.</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;Say your value proposition. So what?&quot; Keep pushing until the answer is specific and credible.</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview Tuesday: &quot;Tomorrow you stress-test your pricing and refine your value proposition with AI and peer feedback.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Value Prop Workshop + Pricing Calculation</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Read your value proposition. One word from the room: compelling or unclear.&quot; Fast round.</p><h4>7:35–8:10 AM — Value Prop Stress Test</h4><p>AI prompt: &quot;Here is my value proposition: [paste]. What objections would a skeptical customer raise? What is the weakest word or phrase?&quot;</p><p>Revise. Then pairs: one reads value prop, partner plays skeptical customer asking &quot;so what?&quot; and &quot;why should I believe that?&quot;</p><h4>8:10–8:45 AM — Pricing Calculation Workshop</h4><p>Work through all three approaches:</p><p>Cost-plus: What does it cost you? What is your margin?</p><p>Value-based: What is the outcome worth to the customer?</p><p>Competitive: What are others charging? Where do you fit?</p><p>Students land on one final price with written rationale: &quot;My price is $[X] because [reasoning].&quot;</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>If a guest speaker or site visit is scheduled this week, spend 10 minutes preparing questions. If not, use the time to refine pricing rationale with a partner.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Competitive Pricing + Revenue Modeling</h3><p>Warm-Up: Would You Rather — Pricing Edition (5 min)</p><p>Post two pricing scenarios. Students choose and defend.</p><p>Charge $5 and sell 200 units OR charge $20 and sell 40 units?</p><p>Offer a free trial to 100 people OR charge $10 to 30 people upfront?</p><p>Debrief: both scenarios can generate the same revenue — pricing is about strategy, not just gut feel.</p><p>Competitive Pricing Analysis (20 min)</p><p>Students research 2-3 competitors and map pricing:</p><p>What do competitors charge?</p><p>What do customers get at each price point?</p><p>Where does my price sit — above, below, or at market rate?</p><p>If above market: what justifies it? If below: is that sustainable?</p><p>Revenue Modeling Intro (20 min)</p><p>Walk through a simple revenue projection: Price × Units Sold = Revenue. If I charge $15 and sell 20 units/month → $300/month → $3,600/year.</p><p>Students build their own monthly revenue model:</p><p>Realistic: how many units can I actually sell this month?</p><p>Goal: what would I need to sell to hit $500/month?</p><p>Stretch: what is the ceiling if everything goes right?</p><p>Closing Reflection (5 min): Exit ticket — &quot;My realistic monthly revenue target is $____ because ____.&quot;</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Pricing Work Day</h3><p>Speaker Recap (15 min)</p><p>If a speaker visited or a site tour occurred this week, debrief:</p><p>What was the most useful thing you heard or saw?</p><p>What surprised you?</p><p>What will you apply to your business?</p><p>If no speaker, open with a 10-minute pricing share-out: two students present their pricing rationale and get class feedback.</p><p>Thank You Cards (10 min)</p><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific thing you learned, one way you are applying it, a genuine thank you.</p><p>Pricing Work Day (25 min)</p><p>Open work time. Students finalize: final price with written rationale, competitive pricing analysis, and monthly revenue model (realistic, goal, stretch).</p><p>1-on-1 focus: any student who has not set a real price yet or whose revenue model is blank. Goal: every student leaves with a price and a revenue target they can defend.</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Profit or Broke + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><p>Warm-Up: Profit or Broke (15 min)</p><p>Present 3-4 business scenarios with revenue and cost numbers. Students vote: profitable or not? Reveal and discuss. Use scenarios close to students&#x27; own business models.</p><p>1-on-1 Check-Ins (25 min)</p><p>What is your price and why?</p><p>What is your realistic monthly revenue target?</p><p>What is the one thing blocking you from selling more?</p><p>Flag any student whose price is clearly unsustainable — too low to cover costs, or too high with no differentiation.</p><p>Week Reflection (10 min)</p><p>This week I figured out…</p><p>My price is $____ because…</p><p>Next week I need to…</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 16</h4><p>Week 16: Validating Key Assumptions. &quot;Next week we test the riskiest things your business depends on — before they become expensive mistakes.&quot;</p>"
