          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><p>WEEK 16 — MONDAY</p><p>Theme: What Is an Assumption? Assumption Mapping</p><p>WARM-UP: Convince Me (8 min)</p><p>Pick a student volunteer. They pitch their business in 60 seconds. The facilitator plays skeptic and challenges every claim: &quot;How do you know people will pay that?&quot; &quot;What makes you think they&#x27;ll find you?&quot; Student must defend or admit they&#x27;re not sure.</p><p>Debrief: every business pitch is full of assumptions. The best founders know which ones are tested and which ones aren&#x27;t.</p><p>MINI-LESSON: ASSUMPTIONS VS. FACTS (10 min)</p><p>Define: An assumption is something you believe to be true but haven&#x27;t proven yet. A fact is something you&#x27;ve tested and confirmed.</p><p>Three types of risky assumptions:</p><p>Desirability: Do customers actually want this?</p><p>Feasibility: Can I actually deliver this?</p><p>Viability: Will this make money?</p><p>Example: &quot;People will pay $25 for my handmade candles&quot; — is that desirability, viability, or both?</p><p>ASSUMPTION MAPPING ACTIVITY (25 min)</p><p>Each student lists 5-7 assumptions their business depends on. Then rank them on two axes:</p><p>How important is this assumption? (1=low, 5=critical)</p><p>How confident are you it&#x27;s true? (1=just guessing, 5=tested and confirmed)</p><p>The highest importance + lowest confidence assumptions are the ones to test first.</p><p>Students circle their top 3 &quot;must test&quot; assumptions and share one with a partner.</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><p>WEEK 16 — TUESDAY</p><p>Theme: Designing Tests — How Do You Validate an Assumption?</p><p>WARM-UP: Assumption or Fact? (5 min)</p><p>Read 5 statements aloud. Students hold up A (assumption) or F (fact):</p><p>&quot;My friend said they&#x27;d buy it.&quot; — A</p><p>&quot;I sold 12 units last month.&quot; — F</p><p>&quot;People my age hate going to the DMV.&quot; — A</p><p>&quot;I interviewed 8 customers and 6 said price was the #1 barrier.&quot; — F</p><p>&quot;My product is better than the competition.&quot; — A</p><p>MINI-LESSON: EXPERIMENT DESIGN (15 min)</p><p>A good validation test has three parts:</p><p>Hypothesis: &quot;I believe [X] is true.&quot;</p><p>Test: &quot;I will [do this thing] to find out.&quot;</p><p>Success metric: &quot;I&#x27;ll know it&#x27;s true if [specific result].&quot;</p><p>Example: &quot;I believe busy parents in our neighborhood will pay $8 for cookie delivery. I will post an Instagram story with a pre-order link. I&#x27;ll know it&#x27;s true if 5+ people click and at least 3 pay a deposit.&quot;</p><p>Common test types: pre-orders, landing pages, surveys (weak), in-person demos, door-to-door sales, social media polls (weak), direct asks with money on the table.</p><p>DESIGN YOUR TEST (25 min)</p><p>Students write a formal experiment design for their #1 assumption:</p><p>Assumption: [what I believe]</p><p>Why it matters: [what breaks if I&#x27;m wrong]</p><p>My test: [exactly what I will do]</p><p>Timeline: [when I will run it]</p><p>Success metric: [what result confirms or denies it]</p><p>Facilitator reviews each design before Wednesday. Flag any test that is too vague or can&#x27;t produce a real answer in 1-2 days.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><p>WEEK 16 — WEDNESDAY</p><p>Theme: Run the Test (or Plan It in Detail)</p><p>WARM-UP: Share Your Experiment Design (10 min)</p><p>Two volunteers share their experiment design from Tuesday. Class gives feedback: Is the test real? Can it be done by Friday? Is the success metric clear?</p><p>TEST DAY (35 min)</p><p>Students run their test — or if the test requires more time, they create a detailed implementation plan:</p><p>Students who can test in class: do it now. Make calls, send texts, set up pre-order forms, do demos in the hallway.</p><p>Students whose test requires more time: build out the full plan — what they&#x27;ll do tonight and tomorrow, who they&#x27;ll reach, what they&#x27;ll say.</p><p>Facilitator tip: push students to get real data, not just opinions. &quot;Would you buy this?&quot; is weaker than &quot;Here&#x27;s the link — want to order one right now?&quot;</p><p>Students who finish early: design a second test for their #2 assumption, OR help a classmate refine their test.</p><p>END-OF-DAY CHECK (5 min)</p><p>Each student states: &quot;By Thursday class I will have tested [assumption] and will have [specific data point] to share.&quot;</p><p>Hold them to it Thursday.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><p>WEEK 16 — THURSDAY</p><p>Theme: Interpret Results + Pivot / Persevere Decision</p><p>RESULTS SHARE-OUT (20 min)</p><p>Each student shares in 60-90 seconds:</p><p>What was my assumption?</p><p>What did I test?</p><p>What happened?</p><p>What does it mean for my business?</p><p>Celebrate honest results — including failed tests. A confirmed wrong assumption is valuable data.</p><p>PIVOT / PERSEVERE / PAUSE (15 min)</p><p>Teach the three responses to validation results:</p><p>Persevere: my assumption was right — keep going and scale it</p><p>Pivot: my assumption was wrong — change one key element and retest</p><p>Pause: I don&#x27;t have enough data yet — I need to run the test again or differently</p><p>Students write their decision for each tested assumption and one concrete next action.</p><p>WEEK 17 PREVIEW + DECK INTRO (15 min)</p><p>Next week is the Unit 2 Final: students build a pitch deck and present to the class using Notre Dame McCloskey Business Plan Competition Round 2 standards.</p><p>Preview the deck structure (8 slides):</p><p>1. Cover — Business name, tagline, founder</p><p>2. Problem — What problem are you solving? For whom?</p><p>3. Solution — What do you offer? Why is it better?</p><p>4. Market — Who is your customer? How big is the market?</p><p>5. Business Model — How do you make money?</p><p>6. Traction — What have you done so far? Sales? Tests? Customers?</p><p>7. Financials — Price, revenue target, costs</p><p>8. Ask — What do you need? What&#x27;s next?</p><p>Students should start their deck this weekend. Monday-Wednesday of Week 17 = build time. Thursday = practice. Friday = pitches.</p><h3>Friday</h3><p>WEEK 16 — FRIDAY</p><p>Theme: Founder Fight + 1-on-1s + Unit 2 Preview</p><p>WARM-UP: Founder Fight (15 min)</p><p>Run Founder Fight. Two student businesses go head-to-head on a judge prompt: &quot;Which business has stronger proof that customers want their product?&quot; Judges vote and explain. Rotate 2-3 rounds.</p><p>Debrief: What kind of evidence is most convincing? Real sales &gt; pre-orders &gt; surveys &gt; &quot;my friend said.&quot;</p><p>1-ON-1 PREP CHECK (25 min)</p><p>Rotate through students while others work on their Week 17 deck start. Focus questions:</p><p>Do you know what your riskiest assumption was? Is it tested?</p><p>Do you have at least one real data point from this week?</p><p>Have you started thinking about your Week 17 deck?</p><p>Flag any student who has no tested assumptions — they will struggle on the Week 17 Traction slide. Give them a weekend assignment: reach out to 5 real potential customers before Monday.</p><p>UNIT 2 REFLECTION (10 min)</p><p>Students write a short Unit 2 reflection (can be collected and returned for Week 17 use):</p><p>The most important thing I learned in Unit 2 was…</p><p>The biggest thing I proved about my business was…</p><p>The biggest unknown I still have going into Week 17 is…</p>"
