          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><p>WEEK 17 — MONDAY</p><p>Theme: Deck Build Day 1 — Slides 1-4</p><p>OPENING (5 min)</p><p>Return Unit 2 reflections from Week 16 Friday. Quick orientation: this week students build a real pitch deck and present Friday. Walk through the 8-slide structure and the 2-minute pitch format.</p><p>Frame it: &quot;This is the Notre Dame McCloskey format. Real founders use this. You&#x27;re going to do it for real.&quot;</p><p>DECK BUILD: SLIDES 1-4 (45 min)</p><p>Students work on slides 1-4 today. Facilitator circulates with coaching questions:</p><p>Slide 1 — Cover:</p><p>Is your business name clear?</p><p>Does your tagline say what you do in one sentence?</p><p>Slide 2 — Problem:</p><p>Who specifically has this problem? (not &quot;everyone&quot;)</p><p>What evidence do you have that it&#x27;s a real problem?</p><p>Slide 3 — Solution:</p><p>What do you actually sell?</p><p>Why is it better than what exists now?</p><p>Slide 4 — Market:</p><p>Who is your ideal customer? Describe them specifically.</p><p>How many of them are there? (use TAM/SAM/SOM or a realistic local estimate)</p><p>End of day: each student should have a rough version of slides 1-4 in a Google Slides or Canva deck.</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><p>WEEK 17 — TUESDAY</p><p>Theme: Deck Build Day 2 — Slides 5-8</p><p>CHECK-IN: Slides 1-4 Status (5 min)</p><p>Quick show of hands: how many have rough versions of all 4 slides? Flag anyone who is behind — pair them with a student who is ahead for peer support during build time.</p><p>DECK BUILD: SLIDES 5-8 (45 min)</p><p>Coaching questions by slide:</p><p>Slide 5 — Business Model:</p><p>How exactly do you make money?</p><p>What&#x27;s your price? Who pays it? How often?</p><p>Slide 6 — Traction:</p><p>What have you actually done? Sales, interviews, tests?</p><p>Numbers are more convincing than stories. &quot;I sold 12 units&quot; beats &quot;people really like it.&quot;</p><p>Slide 7 — Financials:</p><p>Price × units = revenue. Show the math.</p><p>What are your main costs? Are you profitable or working toward it?</p><p>Slide 8 — The Ask:</p><p>What do you need most right now?</p><p>End with a one-sentence vision: &quot;In one year, I want to…&quot;</p><p>End of Tuesday: all 8 slides should have content — even rough. Wednesday is for refining, not building from scratch.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><p>WEEK 17 — WEDNESDAY</p><p>Theme: Full Deck Review + Refinement</p><p>PEER REVIEW ROUND 1 (25 min)</p><p>Students pair up and run through each other&#x27;s full 8-slide deck. Reviewer gives feedback on:</p><p>Clarity: Can I understand your business in 2 minutes?</p><p>Evidence: Where are you making claims you can&#x27;t back up?</p><p>Gaps: Which slide is weakest? What&#x27;s missing?</p><p>Reviewer completes a written feedback form (or sticky notes): one strength, one gap, one question a judge would ask.</p><p>REVISION TIME (20 min)</p><p>Students revise based on peer feedback. Facilitator circulates and flags decks that still have major gaps:</p><p>Traction slide with no real numbers — must get at least one real data point before Thursday</p><p>Financials slide with no actual math — make them calculate it, even roughly</p><p>Problem slide too vague — push for a specific person with a specific pain</p><p>PITCH LOGISTICS BRIEFING (5 min)</p><p>Confirm Friday logistics: order of presentations, timing (2 min pitch + 2 min Q&amp;A per student), screen/projector setup, class Q&amp;A expectations.</p><p>Remind students: no reading from notes during the pitch. Know your deck. The slides are your visual — you are the presenter.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><p>WEEK 17 — THURSDAY</p><p>Theme: Practice Pitches + Feedback</p><p>PRACTICE PITCH ROUND (40 min)</p><p>Every student delivers their full 2-minute pitch at least once. Options depending on class size:</p><p>Small class (under 12): pitch to the whole group, timed, with Q&amp;A</p><p>Larger class: split into groups of 4-5, rotate — each person pitches once and gives feedback twice</p><p>Peer feedback format after each practice pitch:</p><p>One thing that landed well</p><p>One thing to tighten or clarify</p><p>One question you&#x27;d ask as an investor</p><p>Facilitator notes: watch for students who go over 2 minutes, rush through slides too fast, or freeze on Q&amp;A. Coach these specifically.</p><p>FINAL PREP TIME (10 min)</p><p>Students make final edits and practice their opening line one more time. A strong first 15 seconds sets the tone for the whole pitch.</p><p>Homework: practice your pitch out loud two more times tonight. Time yourself.</p><h3>Friday</h3><p>WEEK 17 — FRIDAY: FINAL PITCHES</p><p>Unit 2 Final — McCloskey Round 2 Format</p><p>SETUP (5 min)</p><p>Arrange the room as a pitch space — chairs in an arc or theater-style facing the screen. Remind students of the format: 2 minutes to pitch, 2 minutes of Q&amp;A, then next presenter.</p><p>Set the tone: &quot;Today you are founders pitching to investors. The class is the panel. Ask real questions and give real feedback.&quot;</p><p>FINAL PITCHES (40 min)</p><p>Run presentations in order. Facilitator roles:</p><p>Timekeeper: signal at 90 seconds, cut off firmly at 2:00</p><p>Moderator: prompt class Q&amp;A if silence, protect presenter from pile-ons</p><p>Note-taker: jot one strength and one gap per presenter for post-session written feedback</p><p>Class Q&amp;A prompts if students are quiet:</p><p>&quot;Who is your first customer — describe them.&quot;</p><p>&quot;What would make you give up on this business?&quot;</p><p>&quot;What&#x27;s the one thing that has to be true for this to work?&quot;</p><p>UNIT 2 CELEBRATION + CLOSE (10 min)</p><p>After all pitches: class shoutouts. Each student names one thing a classmate did that impressed them — about their business, their pitch, or their growth over Unit 2.</p><p>Facilitator close: &quot;In Unit 2 you went from idea to something real. You matched a real customer, designed thinking, found your first assumptions, tested them, and just pitched it to an investor panel. Unit 3 is about getting more customers to find you. We start Monday.&quot;</p><p>No game this week — the final pitches were the main event.</p>"
