          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: Segmentation + Message-Market Fit</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students stop trying to sell to &quot;everyone&quot; and learn to focus on a specific segment with a specific message.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Who is your customer? Answer in one sentence.&quot; Around the room. Listen for &quot;everyone&quot; or &quot;anyone who...&quot; — those are the ones to fix this week.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Segmentation</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;The fastest way to fail at marketing is to try to reach everyone. When you talk to everyone, you sound like nothing. When you talk to a specific person about their specific problem, they lean in.&quot;</p><p>What Is Segmentation? (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Segmentation means breaking a big market into smaller groups that share something in common — so you can serve one group really well instead of all groups poorly.&quot;</p><p>Common ways to segment:</p><p>Demographic — age, income, location, job, family stage</p><p>Behavioral — how they buy, how often, what triggers them</p><p>Needs-based — what problem they are trying to solve</p><p>Psychographic — values, lifestyle, identity</p><p>Example: A meal-kit business could segment into (a) busy working parents, (b) college students, (c) seniors who can&#x27;t cook easily. Same product, three very different messages.</p><p>Choosing Your Primary Segment (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;You pick ONE segment to start. The best first segment is where the pain is highest, they are easiest to reach, and they can pay.&quot;</p><p>Highest pain — they feel the problem most</p><p>Easiest to reach — you know where they are</p><p>Ability to pay — they have money for a solution</p><p>&quot;Starting narrow is not thinking small. Amazon started with only books. Facebook started with only Harvard students. Narrow first, then expand.&quot;</p><p>Message-Market Fit (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Once you know your segment, your message has to match. Good messaging names the problem, shows you understand it, and offers relief — in the customer&#x27;s own words.&quot;</p><p>Message formula: Call out the segment → name their specific pain → show the outcome → one clear next step.</p><p>Example: &quot;Busy parents in South Bend: dinner shouldn&#x27;t cost you your whole evening. Fresh local meal kits, on your porch, ready in 20 minutes. Order your first week free.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Segment First Draft</h4><p>Students list every possible segment for their business, then score each on pain / reach / ability to pay. Pick a primary segment.</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;Why that segment first? Could you reach 10 of them this month?&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview: &quot;Tomorrow you write messaging built for that one segment.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Segment Mapping + Message Workshop</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Name your primary segment in 5 words or fewer.&quot; Around the room. The shorter and sharper, the better.</p><h4>7:35–8:00 AM — Segment Profile</h4><p>Students build a one-page profile of their primary segment: where they spend time, what they already buy, what words they use to describe the problem, who they trust.</p><p>AI prompt: &quot;My primary customer segment is [describe]. What words and phrases do they actually use when talking about [the problem]? What messaging would resonate?&quot;</p><h4>8:00–8:45 AM — Message Workshop</h4><p>Students write 3 versions of a core marketing message using the formula: call out → pain → outcome → next step.</p><p>Pairs: partner plays the segment. &quot;Does this sound like it is talking to me? What word feels off?&quot;</p><p>Revise to the strongest version.</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Messaging Work Day</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What we heard&quot; | &quot;Who was this person&#x27;s primary customer segment?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific observation, one question it sparked, a genuine thank you.</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Messaging Work Day</h4><p>Students finalize their core message and write 2 short variations for different channels (e.g. one for social, one for in person). 1-on-1 focus: &quot;Say your message. Who is it for and what do you want them to do?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Founder Fight + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Say your core message in one breath.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Founder Fight (Week 19 — Rotation: Founder Fight)</h4><p>Two students, assigned businesses, 60 seconds each, class votes. Segmentation edition:</p><p>Round 1: A business targeting one narrow niche vs. one targeting a huge broad market</p><p>Round 2: A product with a sharp specific message vs. one with a broad friendly message</p><p>Round 3: A business that knows exactly where its customer is vs. one advertising everywhere</p><p>Debrief: &quot;Which is easier to market and why? What does a sharp message do that a broad one can&#x27;t?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Is your segment narrow enough? Does your message use their words, not yours?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>Who is your primary segment, and why did you choose them first?</p><p>What is your strongest message and who is it for?</p><p>What surprised you about narrowing your focus?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 20</h4><p>Week 20: USP + Storytelling + Brand Strategy. &quot;Next week: what makes you different, and how do you tell a story people remember?&quot;</p>"
