          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — MLK Day / Flex Work Day</h3><p>If MLK Day is observed: NO CLASS. The lecture moves to Tuesday.</p><p>If school is in session: use today for the USP + Storytelling lecture (see Tuesday tab) and run the workshop Tuesday.</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Lecture: USP + Storytelling + Brand Basics</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students can state what makes them different and tell a simple, memorable brand story.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Name a brand you love and one word for why.&quot; Around the room. Note how many answers are about feeling, not features.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: USP + Storytelling</h4><p>Talking Point 1 — Unique Selling Proposition (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Your USP is the one clear reason a customer should choose you over every alternative. Not a list — one sharp thing.&quot;</p><p>Format: &quot;The only [category] that [unique benefit] for [segment].&quot;</p><p>Example: &quot;The only meal kit in South Bend made entirely from local farm produce, delivered same-day.&quot;</p><p>&quot;A good USP is true, specific, and hard for competitors to copy. If your competitor could say the exact same thing, it is not a USP yet.&quot;</p><p>Talking Point 2 — Storytelling (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;People don&#x27;t remember features. They remember stories. The best brand stories make the CUSTOMER the hero — not the founder.&quot;</p><p>Simple story framework:</p><p>A customer has a problem (the struggle)</p><p>They find your business (the guide)</p><p>You give them a plan or product (the solution)</p><p>Their life gets better (the transformation)</p><p>&quot;You are not Luke Skywalker. You are Yoda. The customer is the hero — you are the guide who helps them win.&quot;</p><p>Talking Point 3 — Brand Basics (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;A brand is not a logo. A brand is the feeling people get when they think about you. Three pieces you control:&quot;</p><p>Voice — how you sound (playful? serious? bold? warm?)</p><p>Look — colors, fonts, style that stay consistent</p><p>Promise — what customers can always count on from you</p><p>&quot;Consistency is the whole game. A small brand that shows up the same way every time feels bigger and more trustworthy than a big brand that is all over the place.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — USP + Story Draft</h4><p>Students write their USP (the &quot;only...&quot; format) and a 4-sentence brand story using the framework, with the customer as hero.</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;Could a competitor say your USP word for word? If yes, sharpen it.&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Brand Workshop</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What we heard&quot; | &quot;What is this person&#x27;s brand promise?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific observation, one question it sparked, a genuine thank you.</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Brand Workshop</h4><p>Students define their brand voice (3 words), pick a rough color/look direction, and write their one-sentence brand promise. Optional: use a free tool (Canva) to draft a simple logo or wordmark.</p><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Say your USP and your brand promise. Do they match the segment you chose last week?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Deal or No Deal + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Say your USP: The only ___ that ___ for ___.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Deal or No Deal (Week 20 — Rotation: Deal or No Deal)</h4><p>Brand edition:</p><p>Deal 1: &quot;A designer will make you a beautiful logo and full brand kit for $600 before you have any customers. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><p>Deal 2: &quot;A bigger company will let you sell under their trusted brand name — but you lose your own brand entirely. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><p>Deal 3: &quot;An influencer with your exact audience will post about you once for $250. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Is your USP truly unique? Is your brand voice consistent across your message and story?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What is your USP, and what makes it hard to copy?</p><p>What is your brand promise in one sentence?</p><p>How does your story make the customer the hero?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 21</h4><p>Week 21: Digital Marketing Tools. &quot;Next week we get hands-on with the actual tools — social, landing pages, and content — to put your brand in front of your segment.&quot;</p>"
