          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: CRM + The Follow-Up Game</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students learn to track customers systematically and follow up in a way that builds trust rather than annoyance.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Think of a business that followed up with you in a way that felt good — and one that felt annoying. What was the difference?&quot; 3-4 students share.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: CRM + Follow-Up</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Most sales are lost not because the customer said no — but because nobody followed up. The money is in the follow-up. Today you learn to track people and follow up like a pro.&quot;</p><p>What Is a CRM? (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;CRM stands for Customer Relationship Management. Big companies pay for fancy software. You need a spreadsheet. That is it.&quot;</p><p>A simple CRM tracks, for each person:</p><p>Name + contact info</p><p>How you met / where they came from</p><p>Their stage (lead → prospect → customer → repeat)</p><p>Last contact date + what was said</p><p>Next action + date</p><p>&quot;The single most valuable column is Next Action. It means nobody ever falls through the cracks.&quot;</p><p>The Relationship Stages (6 min)</p><p>Lead (fits your segment) → Prospect (showed interest) → Customer (bought once) → Repeat/Referrer (buys again, tells others). Your CRM moves people from left to right.</p><p>The Follow-Up Game (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Good follow-up gives value every time — it does not just ask &#x27;did you decide yet?&#x27; Each message should give something: helpful info, a story, a reminder of the benefit, or a small offer.&quot;</p><p>The rule of thumb: give 3 times before you ask again. And always make the next step easy.</p><p>Bad follow-up: &quot;Just checking in! Any thoughts??&quot; (3 times in a row)</p><p>Good follow-up: &quot;Saw this and thought of our conversation — [useful thing]. Whenever you&#x27;re ready, here&#x27;s the link.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — CRM First Draft</h4><p>Students set up a CRM spreadsheet and enter every real lead, prospect, and customer they already have — even if it is only 2-3 people. Add a Next Action for each.</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;Who is one person sitting in your CRM with no next action? What should it be?&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview: &quot;Tomorrow you build a full follow-up sequence and start working your list.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Build Your CRM + Outreach Sequence</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;How many people are in your CRM right now? What is your goal number by end of month?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:00 AM — CRM Finalize</h4><p>Students finish their CRM with all current contacts and clear stages. Add a column for source so they learn which channels actually bring people in.</p><h4>8:00–8:45 AM — Value-First Outreach Sequence</h4><p>Students write a 4-message follow-up sequence for a warm lead:</p><p>Message 1 — thank/connect (no ask)</p><p>Message 2 — give value (tip, story, proof)</p><p>Message 3 — give value + soft invite</p><p>Message 4 — clear, easy ask</p><p>Use AI to draft, then edit to sound human. Pairs read each other&#x27;s: &quot;Would this annoy you or help you?&quot;</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Outreach Work Day</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What we heard&quot; | &quot;How does this person keep customers coming back?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific observation, one question it sparked, a genuine thank you.</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Work Your List</h4><p>Students actually send real follow-up messages from their sequence to real people in their CRM, and log it. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;How many messages did you send today? What is your reply rate so far?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Founder Fight + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Any replies from your outreach? What worked?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Founder Fight (Week 22 — Rotation: Founder Fight)</h4><p>Relationship edition:</p><p>Round 1: A business that chases new customers constantly vs. one that focuses on repeat customers</p><p>Round 2: A founder who tracks every lead in a CRM vs. one who keeps it all in their head</p><p>Round 3: A business with 10 loyal customers vs. one with 100 one-time buyers</p><p>Debrief: &quot;Why are repeat customers worth more than new ones? What does a CRM make possible?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Show me your CRM. Who is your hottest lead? What is the next action and when?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What did building a CRM change about how you see your customers?</p><p>What does good follow-up look like vs. annoying follow-up?</p><p>How many people are in your pipeline, and what is your next action?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 23</h4><p>Week 23: AI Campaigns + A/B Testing. &quot;Next week we use AI to build a full campaign and test what actually works.&quot;</p>"
