          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: What Makes a Great Open House Presence</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students know what a strong, professional event presence looks like and start planning theirs.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Think of a booth or table you&#x27;ve walked past at a fair or event. What made you stop — or keep walking?&quot; 3-4 students share.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Event Presence</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;The Open House is your chance to show real people what you&#x27;ve built. Families, community members, maybe future customers or supporters. You have about 5 seconds to earn a stop and 30 seconds to earn a conversation.&quot;</p><p>The 3-Second Rule (6 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;From across the room, someone should instantly get what your business does. That means one bold headline and a clean look — not a wall of text.&quot;</p><p>Big, clear business name + one-line what-you-do</p><p>One strong image or product visible</p><p>Clean, uncluttered table — less is more</p><p>The 30-Second Hook (6 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;When someone stops, you need a quick, friendly hook — not your life story. Problem, solution, and one interesting thing.&quot;</p><p>Example: &quot;Hi! We make fresh meal kits from local farms for busy South Bend families — dinner in 20 minutes, no grocery run. Want to see how it works?&quot;</p><p>Engaging Guests + Capturing Contacts (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Do not stand behind your table on your phone. Stand beside it, make eye contact, and ask a question. And always have a way to capture interest.&quot;</p><p>Open with a question, not a pitch: &quot;Have you ever...?&quot;</p><p>Have a signup sheet, QR code, or form ready</p><p>Give people a reason to leave their info (updates, a freebie, early access)</p><p>Follow up within 48 hours — this is where the CRM comes back in</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Open House Plan</h4><p>Students plan their booth: headline, visual, what they&#x27;ll display or demo, and how they&#x27;ll capture contacts. Sketch the table layout.</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;What does someone see from 10 feet away? What is your 30-second hook?&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview: &quot;Tomorrow you build your actual materials.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Build Booth Materials + Display</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;What is the one visual that will make people stop at your booth?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:45 AM — Build Day</h4><p>Students build their materials: a poster or sign (Canva or by hand), a one-page handout or product card, a signup sheet or QR code, and any product samples or demo.</p><p>Reuse everything they already have: brand (Week 20), message (Week 19), campaign visuals (Weeks 21-24). Nothing here is from scratch.</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;Is your headline readable from across the room? Is your table clean, not cluttered?&quot;</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Pitch Practice + Booth Dry Run</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Give your 30-second hook to the person next to you.&quot; Everyone, at once. Then 3-4 share to the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:50 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Quick thank you cards for Wednesday&#x27;s speaker. Specific, handwritten.</p><h4>7:50–8:50 AM — Booth Dry Run</h4><p>Set up booths around the room. Half the class works their booth, half plays visitors. Then switch.</p><p>Visitors give feedback: &quot;Did I understand your business in 5 seconds? Did the hook make me want to stay? Was it easy to leave my info?&quot;</p><p>Facilitator: play a tough visitor — walk fast, look distracted. See who can earn your stop.</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Fix List</h4><p>Each student writes their top 2 fixes before the real Open House.</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Founder Fight + Final Prep + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;What is the one fix you made to your booth since yesterday?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Founder Fight (Week 25 — Rotation: Founder Fight)</h4><p>Presentation edition — students pitch assigned booths and the class judges which would draw a crowd:</p><p>Round 1: A flashy booth with a weak product vs. a plain booth with a strong product</p><p>Round 2: A founder who talks at visitors vs. one who asks questions</p><p>Round 3: A booth with a clear one-liner vs. one that explains everything at once</p><p>Debrief: &quot;What actually makes people stop, stay, and remember you?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — Final Prep + 1-on-1s</h4><p>Students finalize all materials. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;Are you ready? What is your hook, and how will you capture contacts?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What is your business&#x27;s one-line hook for the Open House?</p><p>How will you capture and follow up with people you meet?</p><p>What are you most excited — or nervous — to show?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 26</h4><p>Week 26: Unit Economics + Revenue Projections. &quot;Marketing is done — now we make the numbers work. Next week: do you actually make money on each sale?&quot;</p>"
