          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: Unit Economics + Revenue vs. Profit</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students can calculate whether they make money on a single sale and understand why revenue is not profit.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;If you sell something for $20, how much of that is actually yours to keep?&quot; Around the room. Most will not know — that is the point.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Unit Economics</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;You can be busy, popular, and selling a lot — and still lose money on every sale. Unit economics is how you make sure that each sale actually helps you, not hurts you.&quot;</p><p>Revenue vs. Profit (6 min)</p><p>Revenue = all the money that comes in. Profit = what is left after costs. &quot;Revenue is vanity. Profit is sanity. You can have $10,000 in sales and $0 in profit if your costs are $10,000.&quot;</p><p>Unit Economics (10 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Unit economics zooms all the way in to ONE sale. If one sale is not profitable, doing more of them just loses money faster.&quot;</p><p>The core math for one unit:</p><p>Price — what the customer pays</p><p>Variable cost — what it costs you to make/deliver that one unit (materials, packaging, fees)</p><p>Contribution margin = Price − Variable cost (what one sale contributes toward your fixed costs and profit)</p><p>Example: Candle sells for $20. Wax, wick, jar, label = $7. Contribution margin = $13 per candle. &quot;Every candle puts $13 toward your rent, your time, and your profit.&quot;</p><p>Fixed vs. Variable Costs (6 min)</p><p>Variable = changes with each sale (materials). Fixed = stays the same no matter how much you sell (a table fee, a subscription, equipment). &quot;You cover fixed costs with the contribution margin from many sales.&quot;</p><p>Revenue Projections (4 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;A projection is an educated guess at future revenue: units × price, month by month. It turns your goal into a plan you can actually track.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Unit Economics Calc</h4><p>Students calculate their own unit economics: price, variable cost per unit, and contribution margin. Be honest about every cost.</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;Did you count packaging? Fees? Your materials? What is your real margin per sale?&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview: &quot;Tomorrow you turn one sale into a 12-month projection.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Unit Economics Calc + Revenue Projection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;What is your contribution margin per sale? One number.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:00 AM — Sharpen the Unit Economics</h4><p>Students double-check every variable cost. AI prompt: &quot;My product is [X], I sell it for $[Y]. Here are my costs: [list]. What costs am I likely forgetting?&quot;</p><h4>8:00–8:45 AM — 12-Month Revenue Projection</h4><p>Students build a simple month-by-month projection in a spreadsheet: units sold, price, revenue, variable costs, contribution, fixed costs, profit.</p><p>Build 3 scenarios: conservative, realistic, and stretch. &quot;Which is most likely, and what would it take to hit the stretch?&quot;</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format. Prompt: &quot;Ask the speaker about their biggest unexpected cost.&quot;</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Numbers Work Day</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What we heard&quot; | &quot;What did this teach me about costs or pricing?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific observation, one question it sparked, a genuine thank you.</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Numbers Work Day</h4><p>Students finalize unit economics and projection in their portfolio. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;What is the one number — price, cost, or volume — that most improves your profit? What would you change?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Deal or No Deal + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;What is your realistic 12-month revenue number?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Deal or No Deal (Week 26 — Rotation: Deal or No Deal)</h4><p>Unit-economics edition:</p><p>Deal 1: &quot;A big customer will buy 200 units — but only at your variable cost plus $1. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><p>Deal 2: &quot;A supplier will cut your per-unit cost 30% if you commit to buying 1,000 units up front. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><p>Deal 3: &quot;You can raise your price 20%. You might lose a third of your customers. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Walk me through one sale. Where does the money go? Is each sale actually profitable?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What is your contribution margin, and were you surprised by it?</p><p>What is the difference between your revenue and your profit?</p><p>What one number would most improve your profitability?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 27</h4><p>Week 27: Cash Flow + Break-Even Analysis. &quot;Next week: when does the money actually arrive, and how many sales until you stop losing money?&quot;</p>"
