          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: The 3 Financial Statements + Budgeting</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students can name the three statements, read a simple income statement, and understand what a budget is for.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;If a friend said &#x27;my business is doing great,&#x27; what is the one number you&#x27;d ask to see to check?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Statements + Budgeting</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Every serious business, from a lemonade stand to Apple, uses the same three reports to understand itself. Today you learn to read them — and it is way simpler than it sounds.&quot;</p><p>The 3 Statements (12 min)</p><p>1. Income Statement (P&amp;L): &quot;Did I make money over a period of time?&quot; Revenue − costs = profit. This is the one you&#x27;ll use most.</p><p>2. Balance Sheet: &quot;What do I own and what do I owe, right now?&quot; Assets = Liabilities + Equity. A snapshot in time.</p><p>3. Cash Flow Statement: &quot;Where did my cash actually move?&quot; (You already built a version of this last week.)</p><p>&quot;Income statement = did I make money. Balance sheet = what am I worth. Cash flow = do I have money right now. Three different questions, three statements.&quot;</p><p>Reading a Simple P&amp;L (8 min)</p><p>Walk through a simple P&amp;L on the board:</p><p>Revenue: $1,000</p><p>Cost of goods sold (variable): −$350</p><p>Gross profit: $650</p><p>Fixed/operating costs: −$400</p><p>Net profit: $250</p><p>&quot;Gross profit is after the cost of making the product. Net profit is after everything. When someone says &#x27;the bottom line,&#x27; this is literally the bottom line.&quot;</p><p>Budgeting (6 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;A budget is a plan for your money before you spend it. Businesses without a budget spend reactively and get surprised. A budget lets you decide on purpose.&quot;</p><p>A simple monthly budget: expected revenue, planned spending by category, and what is left. &quot;If it is not in the budget, you pause before spending.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Read + React</h4><p>Students practice reading 2-3 sample P&amp;Ls the facilitator provides: &quot;Is this business healthy? What is the biggest cost? What would you fix first?&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview: &quot;Tomorrow you build your own P&amp;L and a real monthly budget.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Build a P&amp;L + Monthly Budget</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Gross profit or net profit — which is bigger, and why?&quot; Quick check for understanding. Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:10 AM — Build Your P&amp;L</h4><p>Students build a simple monthly income statement for their business using real or projected numbers: revenue, cost of goods sold, gross profit, fixed costs, net profit.</p><p>If they have real sales data, use it. If not, use their realistic projection from Week 26.</p><h4>8:10–8:45 AM — Monthly Budget</h4><p>Students build a monthly budget: expected revenue at top, then planned spending by category (materials, marketing, fees, savings/reinvestment), then what remains.</p><p>&quot;Give every dollar a job before the month starts. What are you reinvesting to grow?&quot;</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format. Prompt: &quot;Ask the speaker how they decide what to reinvest in.&quot;);</p><p>(Facilitator note: if the prompt above shows a stray character, ignore — just ask the speaker how they decide what to reinvest in.)</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Budget Work Day</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What we heard&quot; | &quot;What would I put in my budget because of it?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific observation, one question it sparked, a genuine thank you.</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Budget Work Day</h4><p>Students finalize P&amp;L and budget in their portfolio, then make one real budget-based decision: &quot;Based on my budget, here is what I can afford to spend on growth this month, and here is what I will spend it on.&quot;</p><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Walk me through your P&amp;L. What is your net profit, and what does your budget say you can reinvest?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Founder Fight + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One thing your budget says you should stop spending on.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Founder Fight (Week 28 — Rotation: Founder Fight)</h4><p>Money-management edition:</p><p>Round 1: A founder who reinvests every dollar into growth vs. one who saves a cash cushion</p><p>Round 2: A business that tracks every number vs. one that &quot;just knows&quot; how it&#x27;s doing</p><p>Round 3: A business that spends big on marketing vs. one that spends big on product quality</p><p>Debrief: &quot;There is no single right answer — it depends on the business. What matters is deciding on purpose, with the numbers in front of you.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Is your budget realistic? What is one category you are over- or under-investing in?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What does your income statement tell you about your business?</p><p>What is one spending decision your budget helped you make?</p><p>Which of the 3 statements matters most to you right now, and why?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 29</h4><p>Week 29: Funding Options + Risk Management. &quot;Next week: where money to grow comes from, and how to protect your business from what could go wrong.&quot;</p>"
