          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: Funding Options + Debt vs. Equity + Risk</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students understand how businesses get funded, the real cost of each option, and how to think about risk before it hits.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;If someone offered you $10,000 for your business today, what would you want to know before saying yes?&quot; 3-4 students share.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Funding + Risk</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;At some point, growing means needing money you don&#x27;t have yet. There are many ways to get it — and every one has a cost. Free money is a myth. Today we learn the trade-offs.&quot;</p><p>The Funding Ladder (10 min)</p><p>Bootstrapping — funding growth from your own sales and savings. Cost: slower. Benefit: you keep full control.</p><p>Friends &amp; family — early support from people who believe in you. Cost: personal relationships on the line.</p><p>Grants &amp; competitions — money you don&#x27;t pay back (like McCloskey). Cost: time to apply; often restricted use.</p><p>Loans (debt) — borrowed money you repay with interest. Cost: you owe it no matter what.</p><p>Investors (equity) — money in exchange for a piece of the business. Cost: you give up ownership and some control.</p><p>Debt vs. Equity (6 min)</p><p>Debt: you borrow money and pay it back with interest. You keep 100% ownership, but you owe it even if the business struggles.</p><p>Equity: you sell a share of your business for money. You never repay it, but that person now owns part of everything you build — forever.</p><p>&quot;Debt is a guest who leaves once you pay them. Equity is a roommate who never moves out. Neither is bad — they are just different.&quot;</p><p>Risk Management (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Every business has risks. Amateurs ignore them. Pros name them out loud and reduce them before they blow up.&quot;</p><p>Common risks and how to reduce them:</p><p>Customer risk — too dependent on one customer. Reduce: get more customers.</p><p>Supply risk — one supplier can cut you off. Reduce: have a backup.</p><p>Money risk — running out of cash. Reduce: keep a cushion, watch cash flow.</p><p>Founder risk — everything depends on you. Reduce: document and build a team.</p><p>&quot;Effectuation&#x27;s affordable loss (Week 9) is risk management: never risk more than you can afford to lose.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Funding Fit First Pass</h4><p>Students answer: &quot;Does my business actually need outside money right now? If yes, which option fits and why? If no, what is my plan to grow from sales?&quot;</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;Many student businesses should bootstrap. Push back on anyone who wants investors without a reason.&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview: &quot;Tomorrow you map your real risks and lock in your funding thinking.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Funding Fit + Risk Map</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Debt or equity — which would you rather take and why?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Funding Decision</h4><p>Students write a one-paragraph funding decision: what they need money for (if anything), how much, which source fits, and the trade-off they accept.</p><p>AI prompt: &quot;My business is [X]. I want to grow by [goal]. Given I am a high school founder, what funding options realistically fit, and what are the trade-offs?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:45 AM — Risk Map</h4><p>Students list their top 4 risks and rate each on likelihood (low/med/high) and impact (low/med/high). For the highest ones, write one action to reduce it.</p><p>&quot;Circle your single biggest risk. That is the one to act on first.&quot;</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format. Prompt: &quot;Ask the speaker how they funded their business and their biggest risk.&quot;</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Work Day</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What we heard&quot; | &quot;How did this person fund or protect their business?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific observation, one question it sparked, a genuine thank you.</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Work Day</h4><p>Students finalize funding decision and risk map in their portfolio. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;What is your biggest risk, and what is your one action to reduce it?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Deal or No Deal + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Does your business need outside money right now — yes or no, and why?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Deal or No Deal (Week 29 — Rotation: Deal or No Deal)</h4><p>Funding edition — this is the perfect week for it:</p><p>Deal 1: &quot;An investor offers $10,000 for 30% of your business. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><p>Follow up: &quot;What does that say you think the whole business is worth? Is it worth giving up a third forever?&quot;</p><p>Deal 2: &quot;A bank offers a $5,000 loan at 12% interest over 2 years. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><p>Follow up: &quot;Can your cash flow handle the monthly payment even in a slow month?&quot;</p><p>Deal 3: &quot;You win a $2,500 grant — but you must spend it only on equipment you don&#x27;t really need yet. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;What is your funding plan, and what is the risk you most need to manage?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What funding option best fits your business right now, and why?</p><p>What is the difference between debt and equity in your own words?</p><p>What is your biggest business risk and your plan to reduce it?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 30</h4><p>Week 30: Legal Structures + Operations. &quot;Next week we make it official: business structures, the basics of staying legal, and how to run smooth day-to-day operations.&quot;</p>"
