          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: Legal Structures + Staying Legal + Operations</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students understand the main business structures, what it takes to stay legal, and why operations systems matter.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Is your business &#x27;official&#x27; yet? What would make it feel official to you?&quot; 3-4 students share.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Legal + Operations</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Two boring-sounding things separate a hobby from a real business: being set up legally, and running on systems instead of chaos. Get these right and everything else gets easier. This is not legal advice — it is a map so you know what to ask.&quot;</p><p>Legal Structures (12 min)</p><p>Write the main options on the board:</p><p>Sole Proprietorship — you ARE the business. Easiest to start, cheapest. Downside: no separation, so you are personally responsible for debts.</p><p>LLC (Limited Liability Company) — separates you from the business, protecting your personal stuff if things go wrong. Most common choice for small businesses.</p><p>Partnership — two or more owners share it. Simple, but you need a clear written agreement to avoid conflict.</p><p>Corporation (C-corp / S-corp) — most complex, built for raising big investment and scaling. Overkill for most starting out.</p><p>&quot;The word that matters most: liability. &#x27;Limited liability&#x27; means if the business owes money or gets sued, they generally can&#x27;t take your personal savings. That protection is why the LLC is so popular.&quot;</p><p>&quot;Most of you would start as a sole prop and move to an LLC once you have real revenue and real risk.&quot;</p><p>Staying Legal (6 min)</p><p>Depending on the business, you may need:</p><p>A business name registration</p><p>A license or permit (food, resale, home business — varies by city/state)</p><p>A way to handle sales tax if you sell products</p><p>Basic records of income and expenses (your P&amp;L and receipts)</p><p>&quot;You don&#x27;t need all of this on day one. But you should know which apply to you so nothing surprises you later.&quot;</p><p>Operations (8 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Operations is just how the work actually gets done, repeatably. When everything lives in your head, the business breaks the moment you&#x27;re busy, sick, or scaling.&quot;</p><p>Say: &quot;A Standard Operating Procedure — an SOP — is a simple step-by-step for a task anyone could follow. &#x27;How I fulfill an order.&#x27; &#x27;How I onboard a customer.&#x27; Boring, and it is exactly what lets a business grow beyond you.&quot;</p><p>&quot;If you got hit by a good problem tomorrow — 50 orders at once — could someone help you if you handed them your notes? SOPs make that a yes.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Structure + Legal Quick Audit</h4><p>Students answer: &quot;Which structure fits me now, and which might I move to later? What licenses or permits, if any, does my type of business need? Where would I look them up?&quot;</p><p>Walk the room. Use AI: &quot;I run a [type] business in [state]. What licenses or permits typically apply, and what structure do similar small businesses usually choose?&quot; (Verify anything important.)</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview: &quot;Tomorrow you pick your structure and write your first real SOP.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Structure Fit + Write an SOP</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Sole prop, LLC, partnership, or corporation — which fits you now? One word.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:00 AM — Structure Decision</h4><p>Students write a short structure decision: &quot;Right now I would operate as a [structure] because [reason]. I would consider switching to [structure] when [trigger].&quot; Plus a list of any permits/licenses to look into.</p><h4>8:00–8:45 AM — Write Your First SOP</h4><p>Students pick their most important repeatable task and write it as a step-by-step SOP anyone could follow.</p><p>Examples: how to fulfill an order start to finish, how to respond to a customer inquiry, how to make one unit of product to standard.</p><p>Test it: pairs swap SOPs. &quot;Could you actually do this task using only these steps? Where would you get stuck?&quot;</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format. Prompt: &quot;Ask the speaker how they keep their business organized as it grows.&quot;</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Operations Work Day</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What we heard&quot; | &quot;What system or SOP could I steal for my business?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific observation, one question it sparked, a genuine thank you.</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Operations Work Day</h4><p>Students finalize structure decision and SOP in their portfolio, and write a short list of the top 3 systems their business still needs. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;If you got 50 orders tomorrow, what would break first? What SOP would prevent it?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Profit or Broke + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;What structure did you choose, and what is the one legal thing you need to look into?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Profit or Broke (Week 30 — Rotation: Profit or Broke)</h4><p>Operations + legal edition:</p><p>Scenario 1: &quot;You skip getting a $50 permit. You get shut down at a market and lose a $300 sales day. Profit or Broke — and was skipping the permit worth it?&quot;</p><p>Scenario 2: &quot;You spend 3 hours writing SOPs. Next month a friend helps you fulfill orders using them, and you handle 2x the volume. Profit or Broke — and what was the 3 hours worth?&quot;</p><p>Scenario 3: &quot;You stay a sole prop and a customer dispute costs you $800 out of your own pocket. Profit or Broke — would an LLC have helped?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Is your business set up to grow beyond you? What is the one system to build next?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What legal structure fits your business, and why?</p><p>What is one legal or permit item you need to look into?</p><p>What is the first SOP you wrote, and what would you systematize next?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 31</h4><p>Week 31: Business Analytics + Data-Driven Decisions. &quot;Next unit we use your numbers and data to make smarter decisions — and start the final stretch toward the year-end showcase.&quot;</p>"
