          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Lecture: Roles + Operational Planning</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students understand the roles a business needs and how to plan the actual work week to week.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;What is the one job in your business you love — and the one you keep avoiding?&quot; 3-4 students share.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Lecture: Roles + Planning</h4><p>Opening (2 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;Back in Week 9 you learned every business needs a Builder, a Seller, and an Operator. Now that your business is real, we get specific: what jobs actually need doing, who does them, and how the work gets planned.&quot;</p><p>The Core Roles (10 min)</p><p>Every business, even a solo one, needs these functions covered:</p><p>Product/Operations — making and delivering the thing</p><p>Sales/Marketing — finding customers and closing them</p><p>Finance/Admin — money, records, logistics</p><p>Leadership — vision, decisions, keeping it all moving</p><p>&quot;If you are solo, you wear all four hats — but you should know which hat you are wearing at any moment, and which one you are weakest in.&quot;</p><p>Founder Trap (6 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;The biggest trap is the founder doing everything — including the stuff they are bad at or hate. That is how businesses stall. Great founders do what only they can do, and get help with the rest.&quot;</p><p>&quot;Help does not have to be employees. It can be a friend, a freelancer, a family member, a tool, or automation.&quot;</p><p>Operational Planning (10 min)</p><p>Say: &quot;An operational plan answers: what work happens, when, and who does it. Most student businesses fail not from bad ideas but from no plan — things just don&#x27;t get done.&quot;</p><p>A simple weekly operating rhythm:</p><p>A set time each week to make/prepare product or service</p><p>A set time to do marketing and outreach</p><p>A set time to handle money and admin</p><p>A short weekly review: what got done, what is next</p><p>&quot;Rhythm beats willpower. When the work has a scheduled home, it actually happens.&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Role Map First Pass</h4><p>Students list every role their business needs, mark which they fill well, which they fill poorly, and which need help. Revisit their Week 9 team map.</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;What is the role you are worst at? Who or what could cover it?&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview: &quot;Tomorrow you build a real weekly operating plan.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Activities: Role Map + Weekly Operating Plan</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Which core role — product, sales, finance, or leadership — is your strongest?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:00 AM — Role Map Finalize</h4><p>Students finalize their role map: every function, who covers it now, and a plan for the gaps (a person, a tool, or an SOP from Week 30).</p><h4>8:00–8:45 AM — Weekly Operating Plan</h4><p>Students build a one-week operating plan: what work happens on which days, roughly how long, and how they&#x27;ll know it got done.</p><p>&quot;Be realistic about your actual schedule — school, sports, life. A plan you can&#x27;t follow is worse than no plan.&quot;</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format. Prompt: &quot;Ask the speaker how they decide what to do themselves vs. hand off.&quot;</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Speaker Recap + Thank You Cards + Planning Work Day</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Speaker Recap</h4><p>Two columns: &quot;What we heard&quot; | &quot;How do they divide up the work in their business?&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:20 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Handwritten. Specific. Required elements: one specific observation, one question it sparked, a genuine thank you.</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Planning Work Day</h4><p>Students finalize role map and weekly operating plan in their portfolio. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;What role most needs help, and what is your realistic plan to cover it?&quot;</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Deal or No Deal + 1-on-1s + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One job you want to hand off first when you can.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Deal or No Deal (Week 32 — Rotation: Deal or No Deal)</h4><p>Team + operations edition:</p><p>Deal 1: &quot;A skilled friend will handle all your marketing for 20% of profit — no cash up front. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><p>Deal 2: &quot;You can hire someone $15/hour to do your least favorite task 5 hours a week. Deal or no deal?&quot;</p><p>Deal 3: &quot;A tool will automate your admin for $25/month, freeing 4 hours a week. Deal or no deal — what is 4 hours worth to you?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — 1-on-1 Sessions</h4><p>1-on-1 focus: &quot;Does your weekly plan fit your real life? What is the first thing that will get skipped, and how do you protect it?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What roles does your business need, and which do you fill?</p><p>What is your weekly operating rhythm?</p><p>What is the first role or task you would get help with?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 33</h4><p>Week 33: End of Year Recap. &quot;Next week we look back at the whole year, measure how far you&#x27;ve come, and start getting ready for the final showcase.&quot;</p>"
