          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Kickoff: What a Showcase-Ready Business Looks Like</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students understand exactly what they are building over the next three weeks and start assembling their snapshot.</p><h4>7:30–7:40 AM — Warm-Up: The Finish Line</h4><p>&quot;In three weeks you present your business to a room of family, community members, and guests. In one sentence — what do you want them to walk away thinking about you?&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:40–8:05 AM — What Showcase-Ready Means</h4><p>Say: &quot;Showcase-ready means someone who has never met you understands your business in 2 minutes and believes it is real. Three pieces get you there:&quot;</p><p>A Business Snapshot — one page that captures the whole business at a glance</p><p>A Showcase Deck — a clean, visual walk-through of your story and results</p><p>A confident spoken presentation — which we rehearse next week</p><p>The Business Snapshot — one page containing:</p><p>Business name + one-line description</p><p>The problem + who has it</p><p>Your solution + what makes it different (USP)</p><p>Traction — real numbers, sales, customers, or tests</p><p>Your ask / what is next</p><h4>8:05–8:50 AM — Snapshot First Draft</h4><p>Students draft their one-page Business Snapshot, pulling from their portfolio. Everything already exists — this is curation, not creation.</p><p>Walk the room: &quot;Could a stranger read this and get your business in 60 seconds? What is missing or fuzzy?&quot;</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Preview: &quot;Tomorrow you finalize the snapshot and polish your brand look.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Build the Business Snapshot + Brand Polish</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Read your one-line business description. Room says: clear or fuzzy.&quot; Fast round.</p><h4>7:35–8:15 AM — Finalize the Snapshot</h4><p>Students finish their Business Snapshot in a clean format (Canva, Docs, or Slides). It should look professional and be printable for the showcase table.</p><p>AI support: &quot;Here is my business snapshot text. Tighten it so a stranger understands my business in 60 seconds, and flag anything vague.&quot;</p><h4>8:15–8:45 AM — Brand Polish</h4><p>Students finalize their brand for public presentation: consistent name, colors, logo/wordmark, and voice across the snapshot and deck. Reuse Week 20 brand work.</p><p>&quot;Consistency is what makes a small business look legit. Same look everywhere.&quot;</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format. If possible, invite a past program alum or the kind of guest who might attend the showcase. Prompt: &quot;Ask the speaker what makes a business presentation memorable.&quot;</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Build the Showcase Deck + Peer Feedback</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One sentence — most interesting thing from Wednesday.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–7:50 AM — Thank You Cards</h4><p>Quick, specific, handwritten thank you cards for Wednesday&#x27;s speaker.</p><h4>7:50–8:35 AM — Build the Showcase Deck</h4><p>Students build a clean showcase deck (evolve the Week 17 McCloskey deck with a full year of results). Suggested flow: Cover → Problem → Solution → Market → Traction/Results → What I Learned → What&#x27;s Next.</p><p>&quot;This is the best version of the deck you have built all year. More proof, more polish, more you.&quot;);</p><h4>8:35–9:00 AM — Peer Feedback</h4><p>Pairs review each other&#x27;s snapshot + deck. Feedback: one thing that is strong, one thing that is unclear, one question a showcase guest would ask.</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Founder Fight + Polish + Week Reflection</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One thing you fixed based on yesterday&#x27;s feedback.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:05 AM — Founder Fight (Week 34 — Rotation: Founder Fight)</h4><p>Showcase-readiness edition:</p><p>Round 1: A gorgeous deck with weak results vs. a plain deck with strong real results</p><p>Round 2: A founder who tells a story vs. one who lists facts</p><p>Round 3: A snapshot a stranger gets in 30 seconds vs. one that needs explaining</p><p>Debrief: &quot;What actually makes a business presentation land with a real audience?&quot;</p><h4>8:05–8:40 AM — Polish + 1-on-1s</h4><p>Students polish snapshot and deck. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;Is your snapshot showcase-ready? What is the one weak slide to fix before rehearsal week?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–8:55 AM — Week Reflection</h4><p>What is the one-line version of your business for the showcase?</p><p>What is your strongest piece of proof?</p><p>What are you most proud to show?</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Preview Week 35</h4><p>Week 35: The End Part 2 — Showcase Prep. &quot;Next week we rehearse until you can present with confidence, and get every detail ready for the big day.&quot;</p>"
