          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Presentation Skills + First Run-Throughs</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Students deliver a first full run-through and learn the presentation basics that make them look and feel confident.</p><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;What scares you most about presenting at the showcase?&quot; Around the room. Name it — it shrinks when spoken.</p><h4>7:35–7:55 AM — Presentation Skills Mini-Lesson</h4><p>Quick, practical coaching:</p><p>Strong open — first 15 seconds hook them (a stat, a question, a story), not &quot;um, so, my business is...&quot;)</p><p>Eyes up — talk to people, not slides. Know your deck so you don&#x27;t read it.</p><p>Slow down — nerves speed you up. Pause. Breathe. Let points land.</p><p>Body — stand tall, hands open, feet planted.</p><p>Strong close — end with your ask and one memorable line, not &quot;yeah, that&#x27;s it.&quot;</p><h4>7:55–8:50 AM — First Run-Throughs</h4><p>Small groups of 4-5. Every student delivers their full presentation once. Group gives feedback: one strength, one fix.</p><p>Facilitator rotates, coaching the specific skills above. Watch for reading off slides and rushing.</p><h4>8:50–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Homework: &quot;Practice your presentation out loud 3 times before tomorrow. Time it.&quot;);</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — Q&amp;A Practice + Booth Setup Plan</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;Deliver your first 15 seconds — your hook — to the person next to you.&quot; Everyone at once, then 3-4 to the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:10 AM — Q&amp;A Gauntlet</h4><p>Students present in small groups; the group fires real questions. Practice handling tough ones calmly.</p><p>Common showcase questions to prepare for:</p><p>&quot;How much money have you actually made?&quot;</p><p>&quot;Who is your customer, exactly?&quot;</p><p>&quot;What was the hardest part?&quot;</p><p>&quot;Is this a real business or a school project?&quot;</p><p>&quot;What is next after this year?&quot;</p><p>Coach: &quot;It is okay to say &#x27;I don&#x27;t know yet, but here&#x27;s how I&#x27;d find out.&#x27; Honesty beats bluffing.&quot;</p><h4>8:10–8:45 AM — Booth Setup Plan</h4><p>Students plan their physical showcase booth: snapshot printed, product/demo, visual/sign, signup or contact method, and how they will greet visitors. Reuse Week 25 Open House skills.</p><h4>8:45–9:00 AM — Speaker Tour Prep</h4><p>Distribute Speaker Tour Prep Worksheets.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Speaker/Tour</h3><p>Run the speaker or tour per standard Wednesday format. Keep it lighter this week if students need the time for rehearsal prep.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Full Dress Rehearsal</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Run the showcase exactly as it will happen next week, so the real thing feels like a repeat.</p><h4>7:30–7:40 AM — Warm-Up + Setup</h4><p>Students set up their booths exactly as planned. Treat it as the real event.</p><h4>7:40–8:40 AM — Dress Rehearsal</h4><p>Run the full showcase format end to end. Half present while half play guests (and invite staff or other classes to walk through if possible). Then switch.</p><p>Facilitator + guest reviewers give each student one specific, kind, useful note.</p><h4>8:40–9:00 AM — Fix List + Thank You Cards</h4><p>Each student writes their top 2 fixes before the showcase. Quick thank you cards for any guest reviewers or Wednesday&#x27;s speaker.</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Deal or No Deal + Final Prep + Confidence Close</h3><h4>7:30–7:35 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One fix you made since the dress rehearsal.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:35–8:00 AM — Deal or No Deal (Week 35 — Rotation: Deal or No Deal)</h4><p>Keep it fun and confidence-building this week. Fast scenarios, high energy — shake off nerves before the big day.</p><h4>8:00–8:40 AM — Final Prep + 1-on-1s</h4><p>Students make final fixes to deck, snapshot, and booth materials. 1-on-1 focus: &quot;Are you ready? What is the one thing you still need to do before the showcase?&quot;</p><h4>8:40–9:00 AM — Confidence Close</h4><p>Go around the room: each student says one sentence — &quot;At the showcase, I want people to see ___.&quot; End on high energy.</p><p>Preview Week 36: &quot;Next week is the Student Showcase. You have done the work all year. Now you show the world. Bring everything — and bring your confidence.&quot;</p>"
