          "weekPlanHtml": "<h3>Monday</h3><h3>Monday — Final Setup + Last Rehearsal</h3><p>Facilitator Goal: Everything is ready and every student has run through their presentation one final time.</p><h4>7:30–7:40 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;One word for how you feel about tomorrow.&quot; Around the room. Normalize the nerves — they mean it matters.</p><h4>7:40–8:20 AM — Final Setup</h4><p>Students finalize and print all materials, load decks onto the right device, assemble booth pieces, and confirm their product/demo is ready.</p><p>Facilitator runs a logistics check: room layout, tables, screens/projector, signage, guest list, schedule, and a run-of-show for the event.</p><h4>8:20–8:55 AM — Last Rehearsal</h4><p>Every student runs their presentation one last time, timed. Quick, encouraging notes only — no big changes now.</p><h4>8:55–9:00 AM — Close</h4><p>Say: &quot;You are ready. Get sleep, dress the part, and show up proud tomorrow.&quot;</p><h3>Tuesday</h3><h3>Tuesday — STUDENT SHOWCASE</h3><p>The culminating event of the year. Students present their businesses to family, community members, and guests.</p><p>Setup (before doors open)</p><p>Students arrive early, set up booths, test tech, and take their places. Facilitator greets guests and sets the tone: this is a celebration of real student businesses.</p><p>Showcase Format</p><p>Choose the format that fits your space and audience:</p><p>Booth-style — guests circulate, students pitch and demo one-on-one (great for products and conversation)</p><p>Stage-style — each student delivers their 2-minute presentation to the full room (great for a formal program)</p><p>Hybrid — short stage presentations, then open booth time for deeper conversation</p><p>Facilitator roles during the event: welcome and frame the showcase, keep time and flow, introduce students if stage-style, and make sure every student gets seen and celebrated.</p><p>Encourage students to capture contacts and interest — the CRM habit does not stop at the showcase.</p><p>Close the Event</p><p>Bring everyone together at the end. Thank guests, recognize the students as a group, and celebrate the year. A short word from a student or two lands beautifully here.</p><h3>Wednesday</h3><h3>Wednesday — Debrief + Follow-Up</h3><h4>7:30–7:45 AM — Warm-Up: How Did It Go?</h4><p>&quot;One high and one thing you learned from the showcase.&quot; Around the room. Let it breathe — this is a big moment landing.</p><h4>7:45–8:20 AM — Showcase Debrief</h4><p>Discuss as a group: What surprised you? What questions did guests ask most? What did people respond to? What would you do differently?</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Follow-Up</h4><p>Students follow up with every contact they made: thank-you messages, promised info, and next steps. &quot;The showcase is a beginning, not an ending. Who do you owe a message?&quot;</p><p>Write thank you notes to guests, judges, and community supporters.</p><h3>Thursday</h3><h3>Thursday — Year-End Reflection + Celebration</h3><h4>7:30–7:40 AM — Warm-Up</h4><p>&quot;The Week 1 version of you vs. the you standing here today — one sentence.&quot; Around the room.</p><h4>7:40–8:20 AM — Full-Year Reflection</h4><p>Students write a final reflection (a keeper for their portfolio):</p><p>The biggest thing I built this year</p><p>The biggest thing I learned about business</p><p>The biggest thing I learned about myself</p><p>What I want to do with this next</p><h4>8:20–9:00 AM — Celebration</h4><p>Celebrate the class. Superlatives, shoutouts, certificates, food — whatever fits your group. Every student is named for something real they did or became this year.</p><h3>Friday</h3><h3>Friday — Closing Circle + What&#x27;s Next</h3><h4>7:30–8:00 AM — Closing Circle</h4><p>Sit in a circle. Each student shares: one thing they are proud of, and one thing they will carry forward. Facilitator goes last.</p><h4>8:00–8:40 AM — What&#x27;s Next</h4><p>Talk through paths forward: keep running the business over summer, next-level programs, competitions (McCloskey and beyond), and how to stay connected to the community they built.</p><p>Students write one commitment: &quot;This summer, I will ___ with my business or my entrepreneurial skills.&quot;</p><h4>8:40–9:00 AM — Final Word</h4><p>Facilitator close: &quot;You started this year thinking entrepreneurship was for other people. You end it as founders who found problems, built solutions, talked to customers, made money, and pitched to a room. That does not go away. Whatever you do next, you now know how to see a problem and start moving. That is the whole thing. Go do it.&quot;);</p>"
